ness: While possessing a driver's license may reflect a degree of responsibility, it does not strongly correlate with financial behaviors relevant to the credit card approval process.\n\n```end\n\nThese steps focus on leveraging domain knowledge to derive meaningful features while eliminating noisy or irrelevant columns.", '### Possible next three steps:\n\nBelow are three proposed feature engineering steps to help improve the predictive performance of the downstream model.\n\n---\n\n```python\n# Age_Bin\n# Binning the "Age" feature into categories (e.g., Teenager, YoungAdult, Adult, Senior) using percentile-based bins.\n# Usefulness: Binning "Age" reduces noise and helps the model focus on age groups instead of granular age values. Certain age groups, such as young adults or middle-aged adults, may have distinct patterns in applying for credit cards.\ndf[\'Age_Bin\'] = pd.cut(df[\'Age\'], bins=[13.75, 22.52, 28.29, 38.31, 74.83], labels=[\'Teenager\', \'YoungAdult\', \'Adult\', \'Senior\'])\n```end\n\n---\n\n```python\n# Debt-to-Income Ratio\n# Creating a new feature by dividing scaled "Debt" by scaled "Income" to get a debt-to-income ratio.\n# Usefulness: This feature captures the financial health of an individual. A high debt-to-income ratio may correlate with lower credit card approvals, while a lower ratio may indicate better fiscal responsibility and higher approval rates.\ndf[\'Debt_to_Income\'] = df[\'Debt\'] / (df[\'Income\'] + 1e-5)  # Adding a small constant to avoid division by zero\n```end\n\n---\n\n```python\n# Industry_Employed Interaction\n# Creating an interaction term between "Industry" and "Employed" to see if employment within certain industries affects credit card approval rates.\n# Usefulness: People employed in certain industries may have a higher/lower likelihood of credit card success. For example, those in Financials or ConsumerStaples might show different approval trends based on employment status.\ndf[\'Industry_Employed\'] = df[\'Industry\'].astype(str) + \'_\' + df[\'Employed\'].astype(str)\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Dropped columns:\n\nBelow is one column that could be dropped based on its irrelevance or redundancy:\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column "ZipCode" is dropped\n# ZipCode is a highly granular feature that is unlikely to correlate strongly with credit card approval success. Additionally, it has high cardinality and does not provide direct financial or demographic insight.\ndf.drop(columns=[\'ZipCode\'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\nBy implementing these feature engineering steps, the model will be equipped with informative and interpretable variables, potentially boosting predictive performance for the task of predicting credit card application success.']
