s and age, where older employed individuals may be seen as more financially stable than younger unemployed individuals.\n# Usefulness: Combines age-related financial stability with employment status to improve the model's ability to capture approval likelihood trends.\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropped column:\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column ZipCode is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['ZipCode'], inplace=True)\n# Usefulness: ZipCode provides location information but does not directly correlate with financial behaviors. Additionally, its high cardinality reduces interpretability and adds unnecessary complexity to the model.\nend", "python\n# Feature 1: Debt-to-Income Ratio\n# Feature description: Calculates the ratio of outstanding Debt to Income, adding a small constant to avoid division by zero.\n# Usefulness: A higher debt-to-income ratio generally reflects financial strain and may lower the likelihood of credit card approval, while a lower ratio indicates better fiscal health.\ndf['Debt_to_Income'] = df['Debt'] / (df['Income'] + 1e-5)\nend\n\npython\n# Feature 2: Employment Duration Ratio\n# Feature description: Computes the ratio of YearsEmployed to Age, representing employment stability relative to life stage.\n# Usefulness: Individuals with longer employment histories relative to their age might appear more financially stable, influencing credit card approval positively.\ndf['Employment_Duration_Ratio'] = df['YearsEmployed'] / (df['Age'] + 1e-5)\nend\n\npython\n# Feature 3: Prior Default Risk Modifier\n# Feature description: Combines the PriorDefault indicator with scaled CreditScore by adding 1 (to maintain non-zero values).\n# Usefulness: Profiles individuals with prior defaults and relatively low credit scores as potentially higher risk, which is important for credit card approval predictions.\ndf['PriorDefault_Risk'] = df['PriorDefault'] * (df['CreditScore'] + 1)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column ZipCode is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['ZipCode'], inplace